# dev/run-test (SPEC, "Building and testing caos, including inside caos"): the
# two small jobs that bracket ONE test.
#
# A test is an ENTRY now — its own directory with a `.caos-expr` and `DEPS`,
# like every std entry — so running it is evaluating it, and almost everything
# this file's predecessor did has no one left to do it for. It used to install
# the tested client, build a git worktree, copy the test's deps into it and
# interpret an exit code; a test that needs a client repo now DEPs on
# `dev/cli-test` and asks for it, and a test that does not needs none of it.
#
# What is left is the two things a test cannot do for itself: turn a tree into
# a running test (evaluation, which a worker may not perform — the worker-side
# `caos` has `eval-path-then` and no `eval-path`), and turn the outcome into the
# report's {verdict, seconds, output}.
#
# A bash worker with the script curried on, not a flake of its own: it is thirty
# lines of shell and the flake existed only to bake a much larger one in as
# `/worker`.
curry --base:@=DEEP-DEPS/bash --worker1:@=worker.sh
